QCA Windows CNG plugin
----------------------

qca-wincrypto provides QCA algorithms through Windows Cryptography API:
Next Generation (CNG, bcrypt.dll). It needs no third-party libraries and
is built only on Windows. Windows 10 or later is the supported target;
the symmetric primitives use CNG, and client TLS uses Schannel and Crypt32.

Provider name: qca-wincrypto

Features
--------

  random                          BCryptGenRandom, system-preferred RNG
  md5 sha1 sha256 sha384 sha512   CNG hashes
  sha3_256 sha3_384 sha3_512      only where CNG has SHA-3 (Windows 11
                                  24H2 and later); detected at run time
  hmac(md5) hmac(sha1) hmac(sha256) hmac(sha384) hmac(sha512)
  pbkdf2(sha1) pbkdf2(sha256) pbkdf2(sha384) pbkdf2(sha512)
                                  BCryptDeriveKeyPBKDF2
  hkdf(sha256)                    RFC 5869 on top of CNG HMAC
  aes128-* aes192-* aes256-*      modes ecb, cbc, cbc-pkcs7, cfb, ofb,
                                  ctr, gcm
  cert                            X.509 DER/PEM import, properties and
                                  chain validation with explicit trust
  tls                             client TLS 1.2 / OS-supported TLS 1.3,
                                  SNI, authenticated records, shutdown,
                                  tls-unique / tls-server-end-point and
                                  TLS 1.3 tls-exporter channel binding
  dtls                            DTLS 1.2, client and server, mutual
                                  certificates, packet MTU, retransmission
  dtls-srtp                       RFC 5764 profile negotiation and export
                                  of directional SRTP master keys/salts
  rsa pkey                        encryption, streaming signatures,
                                  key construction and DER/PEM I/O

AES-GCM with a 96-bit nonce and a 12..16-byte tag uses CNG's native GCM,
including accelerated GHASH. Encryption streams complete blocks and
retains at most a partial block. Decryption retains ciphertext and only
publishes plaintext after authentication at final(). Other GCM parameters
use the constant-time portable GHASH implementation in aesmodes.cpp.
Empty tags are rejected. GCM messages are limited to 2^39 - 256 bits;
the authenticated-decryption buffer must also fit QCA's signed-int API.

CBC encryption uses native CNG chaining, CBC decryption batches AES-ECB,
and CTR batches up to 64 counters per CNG call. Block modes retain only
the final partial/padded block. CFB uses 128-bit feedback and CTR a
128-bit big-endian counter, matching the existing QCA providers. OFB,
CFB and the GCM fallback also use CNG's AES block function.

HKDF keeps intermediate secrets in SecureArray. Cipher temporaries use
a wiping allocator, including allocations discarded when buffers grow.
CNG key and hash/HMAC object buffers are owned by the plugin and use the
same protected QCA allocator as SecureArray, with unique aligned storage.
Native handles are destroyed before these buffers are wiped/freed. Cipher
keys and derived mode state are released at finalization or failure;
completed HMACs retain no native keyed state until QCA calls setup again.
Algorithm object/digest sizes are cached, and HKDF reuses its expansion
buffer. On MSVC x86/x64 secure erasure uses the Windows store intrinsic
instead of a byte loop. These changes add no payload copies or per-update
allocations for key storage. Windows-owned Schannel internals and the
caller's original keys are outside this storage contract.
Provider errors latch the cipher into a failed state; failed operations
do not publish partial output. PKCS#7 padding checks inspect all 16 bytes.

TLS validates chains against the certificates passed to QCA; an empty
trust collection does not inherit system trust. Applications that want
Windows trust should pass QCA::systemStore(), which remains available
without a CRL provider. Native SSL policy checks hostnames, and
peerIdentityResult() must be accepted before exchanging
application data, as with other QCA providers. Trust building is local:
there are no synchronous AIA/root downloads. This provider does not parse
CRLs; CRLs imported by another provider can be checked. Explicit revocation
checks fail if the needed information is unavailable.
Cipher-suite/SSF constraints are enforced before handshake success.
As documented by QCA, startClient() also accepts an empty hostname for
callers that apply their own protocol-specific name checks. In that
mode the plugin still validates the certificate chain; the caller must
check the intended peer before exchanging application data.

TLS 1.3 post-handshake records (including session tickets) are processed
without losing the record state. Stream TLS server mode, stream TLS client
identities, renegotiation and TLS session import remain unsupported.

DTLS negotiates version 1.2 only. It preserves datagram boundaries, fragments
handshakes to the configured MTU (200..65535), retries lost flights with
bounded exponential backoff and handles lost final handshake messages.
Altered records, replayed packets and unencrypted forged alerts do not
publish plaintext. DataChannel application packets must fit the MTU;
the provider does not split one application datagram into multiple messages.

DTLS-SRTP supports SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80,
SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_32, SRTP_AEAD_AES_128_GCM and
SRTP_AEAD_AES_256_GCM. Keys and salts stay in SecureArray; the Windows
export buffer is wiped before FreeContextBuffer. RTP/SRTP packet processing
belongs to the media implementation (for example libSRTP), not this plugin.
The caller must authenticate the peer certificate or fingerprint before
using the material.

RSA supports PKCS#1 v1.5, OAEP-SHA-1 and raw encryption/decryption; the
legacy private-encrypt/public-decrypt operations support v1.5 and raw.
Raw input must contain exactly one modulus-sized representative below N.
SSL-specific v1.5 padding is unsupported. Signatures support PKCS#1 v1.5
with MD5, SHA-1/256/384/512 or raw input, and PSS with SHA-1/256/384/512.
PSS uses MGF1 with the same digest, trailer field 1 and an explicit salt
length (default: digest length). Unsupported descriptors fail closed.
Hashed signatures stream through CNG; raw signing buffers at most k-11
bytes. RSA itself processes one block; bulk data belongs in symmetric
encryption with separately protected keys.

Generation supports 2048..8192 bits in 64-bit steps, exponent 65537.
Component/DER imports accept 512..16384-bit two-prime RSA keys, subject to
CNG validation; small imported keys are for legacy interoperability.
Components and CRT consistency are checked before import. A public view
owns a separate native public handle and never retains a private handle.
RSA public keys can also be extracted from X.509 certificates.

Public export uses SPKI; private export uses PKCS#8. Import additionally
accepts PKCS#1. DER and PEM parsing is bounded (64 KiB DER / 128 KiB PEM),
rejects trailing data and rejects restricted RSA-PSS and multi-prime keys.
Encrypted PKCS#8 uses PBES2/PBKDF2 with AES-128/192/256-CBC or 3DES-CBC.
Export uses HMAC-SHA-1 as specified by QCA's PBE enum, 1,300,000 iterations,
a fresh 16-byte salt and a fresh IV. Import additionally supports SHA-256,
SHA-384 and SHA-512 PRFs, with a 10,000,000-PRF-call work limit. Binary
passphrases, including embedded NULs, are preserved. Select
PBES2_AES256_SHA1 explicitly for AES: QCA's existing PBEDefault selects 3DES.
Single DES, traditional encrypted PEM and PKCS#12 are unsupported.

Private DER, components, plaintext and derived keys use SecureArray.
Native private operations use unnamed CNG handles. The public QCA PEM API
returns a caller-owned QString; callers must manage that plaintext copy's
lifetime. DSA, DH, EC, Ed25519, CSR and CMS remain unsupported.

Schannel identity storage
-------------------------

The original local RSA key is an unnamed, in-memory CNG key. Schannel has
an OS limitation with such keys: its credential signer needs a named key.
For each credential the plugin imports a separate copy into the Windows
user CNG key store under a unique name, then deletes that copy immediately
after AcquireCredentialsHandle has opened its own reference. Temporary
PKCS#8 bytes use SecureArray. Deletion failure aborts credential setup;
RAII also cleans up failures during setup. Certificates stay in memory and
are never installed in Windows trusted stores.

This is not a strictly memory-only identity backend: Windows may briefly
write the temporary key to its protected key storage, and a process crash
between import and deletion can leave an orphan. This requirement affects
DTLS identities, not symmetric keys or ordinary password-based STARTTLS.

Building
--------

Enable the plugin like any other:

  cmake -DBUILD_WITH_QT6=ON -DBUILD_PLUGINS=wincrypto \
        -DCMAKE_DISABLE_FIND_PACKAGE_OpenSSL=TRUE ...

or -DWITH_wincrypto_PLUGIN=yes. With BUILD_PLUGINS=auto or all it is
built on Windows and skipped elsewhere.

Deploy the shared plugin in a discoverable crypto directory, or point
QCA_PLUGIN_PATH at that directory's parent. Static consumers must import
qca_wincrypto. Qt Network selects its own TLS backend independently of QCA;
removing a QCA provider does not select the backend used by Qt HTTPS.
See ../../docs/wincrypto-audit.md for validation notes and limitations.

Regular algorithm tests exercise this provider. WinCrypto additionally
tests streaming, copies, authentication failures, bad padding, KDF limits,
concurrent hashes/MACs and large messages. WinCryptoTLS tests native TLS
1.2/1.3 interoperability, fragmented records, explicit trust, expiration,
hostname mismatch, channel binding, tampering and shutdown. They include
caller-side hostname checking with trusted, untrusted and
expired certificates using QCA's empty-host startup mode. Its local
test server creates a unique temporary Windows user key container and
deletes it on teardown; it never installs trusted certificates.
WinCryptoDTLS covers four SRTP profiles, data-only DTLS, loss/reordering,
small MTUs, forged alerts, bad MACs, replay, a 4 MiB datagram burst, shutdown,
trust/cipher failures and immediate cleanup of temporary CNG keys.
WinCryptoRSA covers key components, all supported paddings/signatures,
16 MiB streaming signatures and clones, plaintext/password DER/PEM,
binary passphrases, malformed input and hostile KDF metadata.

Optional -DWINCRYPTO_TEST_OPENSSL_INTEROP=ON builds an independent OpenSSL
test peer and checks both DTLS roles, datagram traffic and exact RFC 5764
exported keys/salts for all four profiles. It also cross-checks RSA
encryption/signatures and PKCS#8 in both directions, including all four
supported PBKDF2 PRFs. OpenSSL is linked only into this test executable.
The plugin itself still has no OpenSSL dependency.

CI also builds/tests shared and static QCA with wincrypto as the sole
provider and CMake OpenSSL discovery explicitly disabled.
